# Rendering benchmark, not part of the scenario suite (run_scenarios.sh only
# sweeps tests/scenarios/). Run it headfully — a hidden window's swap never
# presents, so frame timings measured headless are fiction:
#
#   GOBAN_SCENARIO_VISIBLE=1 ./cmake-build-release/goban \
#       --script tests/bench/bench.scn --config tests/scenarios/mock.json \
#       --verbosity debug 2>&1 | grep -o "[0-9.]* fps"
#
# Loads a 19x19 position with 140 stones (checkerboard parity, no captures,
# so the layout is deterministic), leaves the game Paused, then renders
# continuously via the MAX_FPS toggle. The per-second averages land in the
# debug log; discard the first sample, it straddles the toggle.
#
# Two traps, both of which have produced published numbers that were fiction:
#
# 1. **Pass --user-settings, one file per run.** With none, a scripted run
#    writes to a single shared `scenario-user.json`, so a sticky setting from
#    the previous run is inherited by this one — and a comparison then measures
#    the same scene twice. Set the state explicitly too (`shader_param X on`,
#    never `toggle`) and assert it with `expect` before measuring.
# 2. **Wait for the camera.** Nothing here does by default, and during the
#    intro flight the board is small and far, so the frame rate is not the one
#    being asked about. `wait_until camera_animating false`.
#
# And measure fullscreen if the answer is meant to describe real use:
# `toggle_fullscreen` before `toggle_fps`. The bowls cost 6.2 ms per frame in a
# 1056x1050 window and 19.4 ms at 1920x1080 — a windowed figure is not a small
# version of the fullscreen one.
load_sgf ./tests/bench/bench19.sgf
wait_idle
navigate_end
wait_idle
wait_until camera_animating false
toggle_fps
wait 7000
quit_when_done
